# These are Linux shell scripts and a Linux kernel config fragment. A CRLF in
# them is a real failure, not a cosmetic one: a shebang gains a trailing \r and
# the script dies with "bad interpreter", and merge_config.sh carries the \r
# into config values. Force LF regardless of anyone's core.autocrlf setting.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.config text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# Same rule, same reason, for everything else that is read by a Linux tool
# rather than by a person: a systemd unit, a GitHub Actions workflow, a Python
# module, a CMake file. None of these tolerate stray carriage returns well, and
# a workflow that fails only on someone else's checkout is a bad way to find out.
*.service text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.cmake text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
